# tmx projects -- copy to ~/.config/tmx/projects on the remote and edit.
#
# This is the file you will change most often. It answers two questions: which
# directories does the picker offer, and what do you want them called?
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO KINDS OF LINE
#
#   ~/code                       a ROOT.  No "=".  Its children are offered.
#   webapi = ~/code/acme/webapi  an ALIAS. Has "=". Names one directory.
#
# That is the whole syntax. Roots shape the menu, aliases give a directory a
# short name of your own. Blank lines and # comments are ignored.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE
#
# Each line below is a CONTAINER. Its subdirectories are offered as work dirs.
# The container itself is not offered, and nothing deeper than one level is.
#
#   ~/code            <- a line in this file
#     api             <- offered, as session code-api
#     web             <- offered, as session code-web
#       frontend      <- NOT offered, too deep
#
# Anything one level too deep is still reachable by typing its path:
#
#   tmx ~/code/web/frontend      opens it, as session web-frontend
#
# So the roots file controls the menu, not what you are allowed to open.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO RULES THAT KEEP THE LIST SHORT
#
# 1. Blocklist. Library, Applications, Desktop, Downloads, Documents, Music,
#    Movies, Pictures, Public, Sites, and anything starting with a dot are never
#    offered. This is what makes bare ~ usable as a root.
#
# 2. Deeper root wins. If a directory is itself listed as a root, it disappears
#    from its parent's listing. This one surprises people, so:
#
#      ~                        ~/code is offered
#      ~/code                   ...no longer offered, because it is a root now.
#                               Its children are offered instead.
#
#    The same thing applies further down. If you add ~/code/acme as a root, then
#    `acme` vanishes from ~/code's listing and you can no longer create a session
#    called code-acme by name. You gain acme-api and acme-web instead. That is a
#    trade, not an improvement, so only add a container as a root when you
#    actually work inside its children rather than in the container itself.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE LAYOUTS, AND WHAT TO WRITE
#
# Everything in one folder:            ~/src/api, ~/src/web
#   ~/src
#   -> offered: src-api, src-web
#
# Grouped by client or org:            ~/code/acme/api, ~/code/globex/web
#   ~/code
#   -> offered: code-acme, code-globex   (the groups, not the projects)
#   Add the groups too if you always work one level deeper:
#   ~/code
#   ~/code/acme
#   ~/code/globex
#   -> offered: acme-api, globex-web     (but no longer code-acme)
#
# Repos loose in your home directory:  ~/dotfiles, ~/notes
#   ~
#   -> offered: dotfiles, notes          (no prefix, straight off $HOME)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECKING A CHANGE
#
# Nothing needs restarting; the file is read on every run. To see the result,
# open the picker with `tmx` and look at the bottom group, which is the work dirs
# that have no session yet. `tmx some-name-that-does-not-exist` also prints the
# names it knows, though the list is truncated.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# ALIASES
#
# An alias becomes the SESSION name, so `tmx webapi` opens a session called
# webapi rather than acme-webapi. Use one when a derived name is not what you
# call the thing, or when the directory sits too deep for a root to reach it.
#
#   webapi          = ~/code/acme/webapi
#   current-project = ~/code/acme/webapi
#
# Two aliases on one directory is fine. There is one session per directory
# whatever it is called, so whichever name you use first creates it and every
# other route attaches to the same session and tells you its name.
#
# Names may use letters, digits, - and _ only, because a name has to work as a
# tmux session name. Anything else is skipped with a warning. An alias also beats
# a same-named live session, so an alias always means its directory.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# A leading ~ is expanded. Roots that do not exist are skipped without complaint,
# so it is safe to keep lines for machines you are not on right now. An alias
# pointing at a missing directory only complains when you use it.

~
~/code

#webapi          = ~/code/acme/webapi
#current-project = ~/code/acme/webapi
